   "text": "\n\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\nTo derive the formula for the sum of the first $n$ squares, denoted as $S_n = \\sum_{k=1}^n k^2$, we will use the **method of telescoping sums** involving the expansion of the cube of a binomial.\n\n### Step 1: Establish the Algebraic Identity\n\nWe start with the binomial expansion for $(k+1)^3$:\n$$ (k+1)^3 = k^3 + 3k^2 + 3k + 1 $$\n\nRearranging this equation to isolate the term $k^2$ (which is the term we want to sum):\n$$ 3k^2 = (k+1)^3 - k^3 - 3k - 1 $$\n\n### Step 2: Summation Over the Range $k=1$ to $n$\n\nWe sum both sides of the equation from $k=1$ to $n$:\n$$ \\sum_{k=1}^n 3k^2 = \\sum_{k=1}^n \\left[ (k+1)^3 - k^3 - 3k - 1 \\right] $$"
